INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l01', 'prog-onboarding', 'Welcome and your first steps', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most apps hand you a long list of buttons on day one. kreators.app starts with a short chat instead. You tell it who you are and what you want, and it switches on the apps that fit. Everything else waits until you need it.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to get from Welcome to Home.
- What the setup chat asks, and why.
- Which apps you get, and how to change them later.
- How to find your way around the tabs.

## Step one: Welcome

When you open the app for the first time, you land on Welcome. It says "Seven relationships. One you." There is one big button.

1. Tap **Start with a short chat**.
2. The app opens a session on your device, so nothing you say gets lost.
3. The setup chat opens. Your guide is Bumling, a friendly grey robot with a gold antenna. Bumling is the app''s own guide, not one of the seven.

If you stop halfway, no problem. Next time the button says **Carry on setting up**, and you pick up where you left off.

## Step two: your account

Bumling asks for your account first, in two small cards.

- **The first card:** your name, your email, and a password typed twice. Then tap **Next**.
- **The second card:** your phone number and your date of birth. You must be 18 or older to join. Nobody else ever sees these two details.
- **The terms box:** it starts unticked. You tick it yourself, then tap **Make my account**.

## Step three: a few questions

Now Bumling asks a few quick questions. Each one comes as small groups of chips. Tap what fits, then tap **Next**.

1. **What you want:** pick up to three goals, like "Learn a new skill", "Meet new people" or "Feel better day to day".
2. **Who you are:** chips like "I''m a student", "I make content" or "I look after family". Pick as many as fit, or tap **None of these**.
3. **What you make:** this one only shows if you make things, for yourself or for clients.
4. **What gets in your way:** chips like "Not enough time" or "Hard to focus (ADHD counts)". You can tap **Nothing, skip**.

After each answer, Bumling says it back in plain words. That way you know it heard you right.

Under every question you will see two small links:

- **Tell me more** explains why the question is there.
- **Change my last answer** takes you one step back.

The typing box at the bottom works at any time. Type "what is School?" and you get a plain answer. Then your choices come back, just as you left them.

> **Pro tip:** be honest about what gets in your way. If time is short or focus is hard, Bumling switches on fewer apps. A short list you use beats a long list you ignore.

## Step four: your apps

Bumling now shows a short list of apps, at most five. Each one has a single line about what it does. Tap **Why these apps?** to see the reason for each one.

- Untick any app you do not want.
- Tap the button, like **Switch on 3 apps**.

The apps that fit go on. Most of the others go off. Nothing is lost: you can switch any app on later.

The last card offers a few next steps. You may see **Go to Home**, an app to open, **Start the strategy session**, **Take the Discover quiz** and **Add my interests**. Pick whichever feels right today.

## Finding your way around

On a phone, the tab bar sits at the bottom with five tabs:

- **Home:** your day at a glance, and the daily brief.
- **Daily:** what to do today, and where you tick it off.
- **Pillars:** your seven relationships, each built as a pyramid.
- **Apps:** every app you have, in groups.
- **Profile:** your tags, your account, your badges and your data.

At the top left sits the kreators.app logo, which takes you Home. At the top right are two buttons: the theme switch, for light or dark, and the apps menu. On a laptop, the tabs and your apps live in a sidebar on the left. You can fold it into a thin row of icons.

The round button with Bumling at the bottom right is the assistant. A later lesson covers it.

## Getting started

For your first two weeks, Home shows a short list called **Getting started**. It has seven small steps, like "Check in on Daily", "Tick your first task" and "Choose your username". Each one ticks itself when you do the real thing. It pays nothing. It only points the way. You can fold it away whenever you like.

## Why it works this way

The app is big. It has apps for learning, earning, money, friends, calm and more. If all of them were on at once, you would not know where to start. The chat keeps your first week small, so you can build a habit before you add more.

## What develops over time

Your first answers are not set in stone. As you change, your apps can change too.

- On the **Profile** tab, **Change my tags and apps** runs the same chat again, with your last answers filled in.
- Under **Profile**, then **Features**, every app has a switch. Apps that suit your answers say "Fits you".
- On the **Apps** tab, open a group to see its apps and switch them.

Your tags help pick your apps. They never show on your public page.

> **Result:** you have an account, a few apps that fit you, and a clear way around. Home is open.

## The bottom line

Start with the chat, keep your first apps few, and let Getting started point the way. You can change your tags and apps any time from Profile.', 1, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l02', 'prog-onboarding', 'The seven pillars and the pyramid', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your life is not one thing. It is work, friends, family, love, yourself, the people who teach you, and what you believe in. When one of them is weak, the others feel it. kreators.app gives each of these a place of its own, called a pillar.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What the seven pillars are, and who guides each one.
- How every pillar is built as a pyramid with six layers.
- What happens when you tick a task.
- How a pillar grows over weeks and months.

## The seven pillars

Open the **Pillars** tab. It is titled **Your seven pillars**, one for each relationship in your life. Each one has a colour and a guide, a small character who looks after it.

- **Servitude**, with Ember, guide to servitude: your work, your money, and the people who count on you.
- **Friends**, with Pip, guide to friends.
- **Family**, with Sunny, guide to family.
- **Love**, with Clover, guide to love.
- **Yourself**, with Dew, guide to yourself: your body, your rest, your mind.
- **Teachers**, with Sage, guide to teachers: the people you learn from.
- **Faith**, with Lumen, guide to faith: your practice and your sense of meaning.

Each guide asks you one plain question. Sunny asks, "Did family hear from you?" Sage asks, "What did you learn on purpose?"

## Why pillars, and not just a to-do list

Most apps start with tasks, and tasks fade in a week. Here, every task hangs from something you care about. When you know why a task matters, it is easier to do on a tired day.

## The pyramid: six layers

Every pillar is a pyramid that stands on its point. The wide layer at the top is the foundation. The small point at the bottom is today''s task.

1. **Foundation:** one thing you will not let break, ever. For Family, it might be "Family gets the first call".
2. **Guiding principle:** a line you live by, so the foundation holds.
3. **Value goal:** how you want to feel more often. No numbers, just the feeling.
4. **Goal outcome:** what you would see when that feeling is real.
5. **Project:** a bunch of tasks with a finish line.
6. **Task:** one small thing you can tick off today.

Read it from the top down and it tells you why. Read it from the bottom up and it tells you how.

> **Pro tip:** keep each task small enough to finish in one go. "Call Mum for ten minutes" works better than "Fix things with family".

## Setting up a pillar

There are two ways in.

- **The strategy session**, on the dark card at the top of Pillars. It sets up the pillars you most need to work on, along with a routine and a plan. The next lesson covers it.
- **One pillar at a time.** Tap a pillar card that says "Not asked yet". Its guide asks you three short questions. Then you see what was built from your answers, and you tap **Set up 1 pillar**. You get a foundation, a guiding principle, a value goal, a goal outcome, a project and three tasks.

If you answered the questions but did not set it up, the pillar''s page offers the same pyramid again. It also has **Or start by hand with a foundation**, so you can write each layer yourself.

## Inside a pillar

Tap a set-up pillar to open its page.

- **Tasks to do** lists the open tasks for this pillar, with **Add a task** under them. A task needs a project to hang from.
- **The pyramid** shows each layer. Open a layer to see what is there, change it, or add to it. A layer only opens for adding after the one above it has something in it.
- A project with a due day shows its pace: **On track**, **Slipping** or **Behind**. That comes from how many of its tasks are done, set against how much time has gone.

Tap any item to change or remove it. Removing asks first, since it takes everything under it too.

## What happens when you tick a task

This is the heart of the app. When you tick a task on Daily, the glow lands on the task and runs up the pyramid to the foundation.

- The task pays **20 xp**.
- A smaller ripple of xp goes to each layer above it: 10 to the project, 8 to the goal outcome, 6 to the value goal, 4 to the guiding principle and 2 to the foundation.
- Clear every task in a project and you get **30 xp** more.

Xp is progress. It fills your level and your pillars. It is never spent. Up to six tasks a day pay xp. The rest still count as done.

## What emerges over time

As you tick, things start to show.

- **Levels:** each pillar card has a ring that fills as its xp grows. Each level needs a bit more than the last.
- **Your weakest pillar:** the one with the least xp sends your small act on Daily, a two-minute task to help it catch up.
- **Badges:** setting up all seven pillars earns the All seven badge. Others come from clearing projects.
- **Patterns:** after a few weeks, the app may notice a task you keep doing, and whether those days went better. It says what went together, never what caused what.

## What develops

The real change is not in the app. It is in you. A strong foundation makes hard weeks easier, and a clear guiding principle makes choices faster. The pyramid simply makes that growth visible.

You do not need all seven at once. Start with one or two. The others wait until you are ready.

> **Result:** you know what each pillar is for, how its six layers fit, and why a small tick today makes the whole pyramid stronger.

## The bottom line

Seven pillars, one for each relationship. Each is a pyramid, from the foundation at the top to today''s task at the point. Tick the task, and the glow runs all the way up.', 2, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l03', 'prog-onboarding', 'The strategy session and your life map', 'lesson', NULL, 'Some days you want the whole picture: where each relationship stands, what drags you down, and what to work on first. That is what the strategy session is for. It takes a few minutes, and you leave with a map, a routine and a plan.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How the session places all seven relationships on a map.
- What your core, your floor and the Courage line mean.
- How your focus, routine and contract are built.
- Where your life map lives, and how to run it again.

## Starting the session

On the **Pillars** tab, tap the dark card **Set up your pillars: the strategy session**. It opens as a chat, like the setup chat. Bumling hosts most of it. Lumen, guide to faith, hosts the part about practices. Each pillar''s own guide asks that pillar''s questions.

The header has **Start over** and **Close**. What you finish stays saved, so you can come back later.

## Part one: your map

For each relationship, you see a list of feelings, from Shame and Fear up through Courage to Oneness. Each one comes with how it sounds in your head, like "I''m scared and I''m doing it anyway" for Courage.

1. Tick every feeling you have in that relationship, on good days and on bad ones.
2. If words come easier than lists, open **Say it in your own words**. Type how it feels, then tap **Read my words**. It suggests feelings, and you choose which to tick. With a plan, **Read with AI** does the same with a model.
3. Love, Teachers and Faith have a switch, **Not part of my life right now**. Set one aside and it stays out of your focus and gets no pyramid.

When all seven are done, tap **That is my map**.

## Core, floor and Courage

From your ticks, the app finds two points for each relationship.

- **Your core:** where you usually live. It is where most of your ticks sit.
- **Your floor:** where you drop under stress. It is the lowest feeling you ticked.

Courage is the line that matters most. Below it, fear, blame or shame run the show. At Courage and above, you show up even when it is hard.

- **A floor under Courage** marks that relationship as a good place to start.
- **A big drop**, more than 150 between floor and core, gets a note to check it weekly.
- **A core at Inner light or higher** asks for proof: one thing you did last week that shows it. Without it, the core is placed at Acceptance.

> **Pro tip:** tick the hard feelings too. A map that only shows good days cannot help you on bad ones.

## Part two: what gets in the way, and your scores

Next, you name your fears and insecurities, each tied to a pillar, and pick how heavy each feels. Naming them early lets the plan answer them.

Then comes a scorecard with stars, from Very poor to Great, for your health, your wealth and the seven relationships.

## Part three: your real day

You say when you wake up, when the lights go out, and your work hours. The routine is built from this real day, not a perfect one.

## Part four: your focus

Bumling shows your map and suggests up to two relationships to work on first. Floors under Courage come first. You can keep the picks or choose your own, then tap **Work on these**. Two at a time works better than seven at once.

## Part five: routine, practices and the contract

- **Your routine:** six blocks for the day. They are the morning routine, food and water, work, people, learning and reflection, and lights out. Only the morning has steps to tick. Each week you keep your deal, wake-up moves thirty minutes earlier, toward 6:30.
- **Practices:** Lumen offers a mantra, a breathing exercise, a line to say and something to read. You can use your own words, or none for now.
- **The contract:** how many days a week you will keep the routine (five to start), a check-in time, and what a short week costs. You pick from three: the streak breaks, unlocks wait, or the coach sees it. You sign by typing your name, then tap **Sign it**.

On your check-in time, a reminder lands in your inbox on any day you have not checked in.

## Part six: your pillars

Now each focus pillar''s guide asks its short questions. A question with choices also has **Write my own**. Then one card lets you pick the value goal and project for each pillar. Tap **Build my pillars**, and the pyramids are made. The chat ends with **Open my pillars**, or **Add my interests** first.

## Your life map

Everything the session found is kept on the Pillars tab, under the pillar list, as **Your life map**.

- **Month one:** your focus pillars.
- **Where each one sits:** a bar from floor to core for each relationship, with a thin line for Courage.
- **Day zero scores**, **What gets in the way**, **Your routine** and **The contract**.

You can change the routine right there. Each pillar card above it also shows its line from floor to core.

## What emerges and what develops

The first map is a starting point, not a grade. Over weeks, your routine streak grows, wake-up moves earlier, and your focus pillars fill with xp. When you are ready, tap **Run the session again** and place yourself afresh. The real growth is a floor that rises: dropping less far on hard days.

> **Result:** you have a map of all seven relationships, a focus of one or two, a routine from your real day, and a signed plan.

## The bottom line

The strategy session shows where you usually live and where you drop. It points you to start where the floor is lowest, and builds a routine and a contract around your real day. Run it again whenever you want to see how far you have come.', 3, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l04', 'prog-onboarding', 'Daily, part one: tasks and your week', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your pillars say why. Daily says what, today. It is where your plan turns into ticks. This lesson covers the top of Daily and your tasks. The next one covers habits, the not to do list, reminders and your routine.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to check in and do your small act.
- How to plan a task with a do date, a due date, a priority and an area of life.
- How to sort the list, add a task, and use the month calendar.
- How Plan my week spreads your tasks so no day is too full, and gives each one a block.

## The top of Daily

Daily opens with **Show up**. Tap **I showed up today** to check in. It pays 10 xp and 10 points and keeps your streak going. Every seventh day in a row pays 50 bonus points. Once you tap it, the row reads "Checked in, day" and your streak count.

Next is your guide''s small act for today, like "Dew''s small act for today". It is one two-minute thing for your weakest pillar, and it pays 15 xp.

## Your tasks

Under **Tasks** you see what is open for today, from the pillars you set up. Tap the box to tick one. It pays 20 xp and sends a ripple up its pyramid.

Your first three ticks have no wait. After that, a task must be ten minutes old, and ten minutes after your last tick. So you do the thing first, then tick it. Up to six tasks a day pay xp. The rest still count as done.

## Planning a task

Tap the pencil beside a task. Its plan opens right under it.

1. **Do on:** the do date, the day you will do it. The task shows on Daily from that day, and cannot be ticked before. **Today**, **Tomorrow** and **Next week** are quick picks.
2. **Due by:** the deadline. After it the task shows Overdue, but you can still tick it.
3. **Priority:** P0 Do now, P1 Important, P2 Normal or P3 Whenever. It helps you choose and sort. It never changes what a tick pays.
4. **Area of life:** No area, or Physical, Financial, Mental, Emotional or Spiritual. The task wears that tag, and a tick counts toward that area in Life.
5. **Time block:** leave it on By its time, or pick a block of your day, like Deep work. Part three of Daily covers blocks.
6. **Time** and **Minutes:** when you will do it, and how long it should take. Quick picks run from 15 minutes to an hour and a half.
7. **Subtasks:** small steps inside the task, up to twenty. They pay nothing and never stop you ticking the task.

Tap **Save plan**. Once a task has minutes, **Start focus** opens Mindfulness with the task picked and the timer set to that length.

> **Pro tip:** the do date and the due date are not the same thing. The do date is when you will work on it. The due date is when it must be done. The do date can never come after the due date.

## Sorting the list

- **Kind of day:** a dropdown that starts on **Follow your week**, with the day template your week gives today, like Everyday. Pick another, like Deep work day, Family day or Low energy day, to change today only. Your kind of day is a day template. Part three of Daily covers them.
- **Area:** shows once a task has an area. Pick one area, every area, or No area.
- **Sort by:** Day, Priority, Due date, By context, By time block or By area. By context keeps P0 on top. Inside each priority, it puts first the pillar your day template leads with.
- **By time block** and **By area** show your tasks in coloured boxes you can open and fold, with P0 first in each box.

## Adding a task

Under the list, open **Add a task**. Type the task, pick which project it holds up, and add it. A line under it says where it went. A task always hangs from a project, so it always has a reason.

## The lists below

- **Not scheduled yet:** tasks with no do date and no due date. They stay out of Tasks until you pick a day. Then they move up.
- **Done today:** what you ticked, struck through, with the time. Tap one to untick it. That takes back its xp.

## The month calendar

Open **Month calendar** under the Tasks heading.

- Each day shows how many tasks are still to do, or a tick when all are done.
- Tap a day to see its tasks, tick them, or **Add a task on this day**.
- Each day wears a small mark in its kind of day''s colour. In the day''s sheet, **Kind of day** sets the kind for that date, past or future. **Follow your week** puts it back.
- **Print this month** gives you a clean paper copy.

## Plan my week

Beside the Tasks heading sits **Plan my week**. It looks at your open tasks with no day and suggests a day for each, in the next seven days, and a time block too.

1. Set **Hours of planned work a day**. It starts at four.
2. Higher priority tasks are placed first, each on the lightest day it fits.
3. **Plan strength** says Strong, Fair or Light. Days with something planned, days that are not overloaded, and tasks with a length all help.
4. **Worth a look** lists anything to fix, like a heavy day, a block that is too full, or a task with no length. Each line has a **Fix**.
5. Each suggestion shows its block under the title, picked from the task''s area of life and pillar. A select beside it changes the block.
6. **Add your own** finds any open task by name and plans it here too.
7. The gear at the top opens **Time blocks**, where you shape your days.

Nothing changes until you tap **Keep what fits**. **Skip them all** leaves your week as it was.

## Why it works this way

A list that grows forever stops being useful. Do dates keep today short. Due dates keep deadlines in view without holding you back. The wait between ticks keeps the xp honest.

## What emerges and what develops

After a few weeks, Daily starts to reflect you back. Your streak grows and your pillars level up. On Home, the daily brief notes when you ticked more than the week before, or when tasks run late.

What develops is a skill: planning a day you can actually finish. That small skill, kept up, is what moves the bigger things in your life.

> **Result:** you check in, do your small act, plan your tasks with clear dates, and lay out a week that fits.

## The bottom line

Daily is three small things: check in, do the small act, tick a task. Give each task a do date, a priority and, if you like, an area. Let Plan my week spread the rest.', 4, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l05', 'prog-onboarding', 'Daily, part two: habits, reminders and your routine', 'lesson', NULL, 'A good day is what you do again and again, and what you stay away from. This lesson covers the parts of Daily that work across many days: repeats and habits, the not to do list, Remember, water and reminders, and your routine.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to make a task repeat, and when it counts as a habit.
- How the not to do list and its streaks work.
- How Remember, water and your own reminders reach you.
- How your morning routine fits under Show up.

## Making a task repeat

Open a task''s plan with the pencil. Under **Repeat**, pick Every day, Weekdays, Every week, Every month or Every year, or every two weeks, say. For a weekly task, pick the days. **Stop after** is an optional last day.

A repeat needs a do date or a due date to count from. When you tick the task, the next one appears by itself, with the same words, time and length. If you untick it, the new one goes away again.

A monthly task that starts on the 31st lands on the last day of a short month, then goes back to the 31st.

## Habits

While a repeat is set, the plan shows **Count it as a habit**. It is on to start, and the task wears the **Habit** tag. Turn it off if the task only comes back and is not really a habit, like paying a bill.

Further down Daily sits a section called **Habits**. Here the app sets a task you keep doing beside how those days went. It needs a few weeks of ticks, and enough days with the task and without it.

- If those days went clearly better, the task shows as a **Good habit**. You might see **Make it a habit**, which sets up a repeat.
- If they went clearly worse, it shows as a **Bad habit**. It can offer **Add to not to do**.
- If it is too soon to tell, it waits and keeps watching.

"Better" is read from your mood, sleep and stress, and from your other tasks and focus time, only from the apps you have on. The line always says things went together. It never says one caused the other.

> **Pro tip:** log your mood or sleep for a few weeks, even roughly. The more days you log, the sooner Habits can tell a good habit from a bad one.

## Not to do

The **Not to do** list holds the things you are staying away from, like "No phone in bed". You can keep up to seven.

1. Tap **Add one**, type it, and pick a pillar or leave it on Any pillar. Tap **Add**.
2. Each day, tick each one you kept. A kept day pays 5 xp and grows its streak.
3. The three-dot button opens the row in place. It shows your best run, **I slipped today** and **Remove from the list**.

A slip or a missed day starts the streak over. Removing an item asks first. The xp you earned stays. The same list shows on the Pillars tab, under your seven pillars.

## Remember

In a task''s plan, **Remember** is a tag for things you want to keep in mind.

- **With a day:** one note lands in your inbox on that day, at the task''s time, or at 9:00 when it has none.
- **With no day:** the task waits in **To remember** on Daily, always in sight. Pick a day to move it into Tasks, with its inbox note.

## Water and your reminders

On a phone, Daily has a **Water and reminders** section. Tap **+ Glass** each time you drink. On a laptop, the same panel opens from a small bubble at the left of the screen.

Open it and you get one card with three round buttons: Water, Reminders and Settings.

- **Water:** a glass fills toward your daily goal. Pick an amount and add it, or undo the last one. Below it, the last seven days show which days met the goal.
- **Settings:** **Remind me to drink** sends a note to your inbox until the goal is met, every two or four hours, between the times you pick. You also set your daily goal and the size of your glass.
- **Reminders:** your own reminders, like "Stretch for five minutes". Each has a time and the days it repeats. You can keep up to twelve.

Water pays no xp. It is there to help you keep one simple goal.

## Your routine under Show up

If you did the strategy session, **Your routine** sits right under Show up.

- The dots show this week, and the line says how many days you kept against your deal.
- Pick **Normal day** or **Exceptional day**, for days that do not follow the usual shape.
- Tick each morning step as you do it. Each one shows its minutes. The day counts only when every morning step is ticked.
- The rest of the day is shown as a timeline of blocks, like work, people and lights out.

If you have not done the session yet, this spot offers **Run the strategy session** instead.

## Why it works this way

Willpower comes and goes. Repeats, reminders and streaks carry you on the days it is low. What you stay away from matters as much as what you do.

## What emerges and what develops

Over weeks, streaks build, and Habits starts to show which tasks lift your days. Wake-up moves a little earlier each week you keep the deal. What develops is a calmer default: you keep promises to yourself without having to think about it.

> **Result:** your good tasks repeat by themselves, the things you avoid have streaks, and reminders reach you at the right time.

## The bottom line

Make the tasks that matter repeat, keep a short not to do list, and let Remember and your reminders do the remembering. Then tick your morning routine and let the streaks grow.', 5, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l18', 'prog-onboarding', 'Daily, part three: time blocks and the shape of your day', 'lesson', NULL, 'A list of tasks says what to do. It does not say when. Time blocks give your day a shape, so each task has a place to land. This lesson covers blocks, day templates, your usual week, and how tasks fall into blocks.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What a time block is, and how to make one in your own colour.
- How a day template sets your kind of day.
- How your usual week works, and how to change just one day.
- How tasks fall into blocks, and how to see them in coloured boxes on Daily.
- How Plan my week suggests a block for each task.

## Where to find it

On Daily, tap **Time blocks** beside the Tasks heading. The same page opens from **Edit kind of day** under the sort row, and from the gear at the top of Plan my week. It is part of Daily, so it is always on. Nothing on it costs points or credits.

## Blocks

A time block is a named stretch of your day, like Deep work or Sleep. Each block has:

- **A name**, like Deep work.
- **A tag**, a word or two that groups blocks, like Focus, Admin, Self care or Recovery. You can type your own.
- **A colour.** Its boxes and bands on Daily wear it.
- **An area of life and a pillar**, if you like. They help the app suggest a block for a task.

You start with 14 blocks. Ten of them make up a normal day, from Morning hygiene at 7 am to Sleep at 11 pm. Four more, Travel, Family time, Date and Reflection, are used by some of the day templates. You can keep up to 24.

Under **Blocks**, tap a block to change it, then tap **Save**. **Add a block** makes a new one. **Arrange** lets you change their order. To delete a block, open it and tap the bin beside **Save**. It asks first. Your tasks stay; they just lose that block.

## Your own colour

The colour row holds eleven colours round a wheel, from amber to lime. The last button, with a pipette, is your own colour.

1. Tap the pipette. A small card opens just under it.
2. Type a hex code like 1E90FF, or a colour name like teal. A dot shows how it looks.
3. Tap the tick. The button now wears your colour, and so does the block.

Tap the pipette again any time to change it.

## Day templates: your kind of day

A day template lays your blocks on the clock. Deep work from 10 am to 1 pm, Recovery from 1 pm to 3 pm, and so on. It also names the pillar that leads the day, and a second one.

A day template is your kind of day. You start with 14, like Everyday, Deep work day, Family day, Recovery day, Low energy day and Travel day. Each one is a starting point. Change anything.

Tap a template under **Day templates** to open it.

1. Change its name, **Pillar that leads** and **Second pillar**.
2. Each slot is a block with a start and an end. An end before the start runs past midnight, like Sleep from 11 pm to 7 am.
3. Slots can never overlap. A gap is fine and shows as free time.
4. Tap **Save**, or **Save as new** to keep a copy.

The three dots hold **Make it the default**, **Copy** and **Delete**. You can keep up to 24 templates. Your default cannot be deleted until another one is the default.

> **Pro tip:** do not build a perfect week on day one. Use Everyday for a week. Then change only the block that kept going wrong.

## Your usual week, and one-day changes

Every day starts on your default template, **Everyday**. Under **Your usual week**, open **Set each weekday** to give a weekday its own template. Say Sunday is Family day. Each change saves at once.

Some days do not fit the pattern. A trip, a sick day, a launch. Change just that day, and your week stays as it was:

- **On Daily**, the **Kind of day** dropdown starts on **Follow your week**. Pick another template, and it is for that date only.
- **In the month calendar on Daily**, each day shows the colour of its kind. Tap a day, past or future, and pick its **Kind of day** in the sheet. **Follow your week** clears it. The sheet also says whether the day was set for this day only or comes from your usual week.
- **On the Time blocks page**, tap a day under **This week**. You see its blocks in order, with the free time between. Its **Kind of day** works the same way. A changed day wears a **Changed** tag.
- **Use it on more days** puts that template on other weekdays. Pick **Every week** to change your pattern, or **This week only** for just those dates. Then tap the **Apply** button, which says how many days.

Pick **Follow your week** again to undo a one-day change.

**This month** on the Time blocks page counts how many days of each kind you have had so far this month. Days on Everyday are not counted.

## How tasks fall into blocks

A task finds its block in one of three ways.

1. **You pick it.** In a task''s plan, the **Time block** select lists the blocks of that day first, then your other blocks.
2. **By its time.** Leave it on **By its time**, and a task at 11 am sits in the block that covers 11 am.
3. **Plan my week suggests one**, from the task''s area of life and pillar.

## Seeing your day in blocks

On Daily, **Sort by** now has six choices: Day, Priority, Due date, By context, By time block and By area.

- **By time block** shows your tasks in coloured boxes, one per block, in clock order. Each box shows its times and a count, like "3 tasks · 1 h 30 of 3 h". Tasks with no block sit in **No block yet**.
- **By area** shows a box for each area of life that has tasks, in the order physical, financial, mental, emotional and spiritual. Tasks with no area sit in **No area**.
- Inside every box, P0 comes first.
- Tap a box to fold it. **Open all** and **Fold all** sit above them.
- These two views show up to 60 tasks, not six, so you can see the whole day.

**By context** reads the pillar your day template leads with. On a Family day, family tasks come first inside each priority. On a day that follows your default, the list sorts by day, unless you pick another sort.

Just above the list, **Your day** is a fold that shows each block as a band, with its tasks inside. It says how much room is left, like "45 min free", or "Over by 30 min" when you planned too much.

## Plan my week suggests a block

Plan my week now gives each task a day and a block. It leans on the block''s area and pillar:

- A task in the financial area goes to a financial block, like Deep work or Busy work. A task with no area goes by its pillar.
- P0 and P1 tasks lean to a block tagged Focus. P2 and P3 tasks lean to the others.
- A block with room left wins over a full one.

Each suggestion shows its block under the title, with a select to change it. **Worth a look** adds two new lines. One says when a block is too full, like "Deep work on Tue holds 4h 30m of 3h." The other says when a Focus block is empty, and its **Fix** lets you drop a task in. Nothing changes until you tap **Keep what fits**.

## Why it works this way

A day has only so many hours. When you see the day as blocks, you see the room before you fill it. A day template saves you from planning the same shape again and again. And a one-day change means a bad day never breaks your week.

## What emerges and what develops

After a few weeks, Emerging on Daily starts to read your blocks back to you. It may say "You ticked 14 of 20 Deep work tasks inside the block." It also sets the tasks you tick on one kind of day beside your other days.

**Your kinds of day** shows your last four weeks by kind, each in the colour of its longest block, and how each kind went beside your other days: tasks ticked, and focus minutes and mood while those apps are on. A day counts as the kind you set for it, else the one your week gives it. Days on Everyday count as no kind. You see it on Daily, on the Time blocks page, under Emerging and on Reviews. Each line says what went together, never what caused what.

What develops is a sense of your own rhythm. You learn which hours are for deep work and which are for rest, and you plan around them.

> **Result:** your day has a shape. Each weekday has its kind of day, one-off days are easy to change, and your tasks fall into the blocks where they fit.

## The bottom line

Blocks are the parts of your day. A day template puts them on the clock and is your kind of day. Your usual week picks one for each weekday, and one date can change without touching the rest. Let tasks fall into blocks, and sort By time block to see where your day goes.', 6, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l06', 'prog-onboarding', 'Home: your day on one screen', 'lesson', NULL, 'You open the app with a busy day ahead. You do not want to hunt through ten apps to see where you stand. Home puts the day on one screen. It shows what changed, what to do next, and the numbers that tell you how you are doing.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What sits at the top of Home, and why it comes in that order.
- How the daily brief and Getting started point the way.
- How Breathe and focus and the line of the day fit in.
- How to read your xp, your level and your Life score.

## Why Home is there

Every app in kreators.app does one job. Home is where they meet. It reads your own numbers and shows them back in a calm order: first where you stand, then what to do.

## The header

Home opens with a dark card. It shows the date and a greeting with your first name. Under the name are three small links:

- **Your level:** opens your pillars.
- **Your streak:** how many days in a row you checked in. It opens Daily.
- **Your mood:** today''s face, or "Log your mood" if you have not picked one.

Beside them sit **My page**, your public page, and a bell for your Inbox. Your picture opens the Profile tab. **Search anything** sits at the foot of the card, with results right under it as you type.

## Water, then a line for the day

Next comes **Water and reminders**. Tap **+ Glass** each time you drink. Three round buttons switch the card between **Water**, **Reminders** and **Settings**, where you add your own reminders and set your goal.

Under it sits the line of the day. It takes turns: a line from a philosopher one day, then a line written for the app the next. Every line shows once before any comes back. Tap the round **Another line** button to see a new one. It then takes turns between a tip, a small thing to do today, and a line to think about.

## Breathe and focus

Next is **Breathe and focus**, the same panel you find in Mindfulness. It has four tabs: **Breathe**, **Timer**, **Pomodoro** and **Stopwatch**. The Mindfulness lesson covers it in full.

## Your daily brief

**Your daily brief** shows two short lists:

- **What changed:** three things that moved in your own numbers.
- **Next steps:** three small things to do next.

The brief runs on plain rules, not AI, so it never costs credits. It never points at an app you have switched off. If you take the Discover quiz and allow it, the steps start to fit how you work.

## Getting started

A new account also sees **Getting started** for its first 14 days. It is a list of seven first steps:

- Check in on Daily.
- Tick your first task.
- Do a small act.
- Pick two interests.
- Choose your username.
- Join a circle, an event or a run club.
- Take the strategy session.

You cannot tick them by hand. Each one ticks itself when you do the thing. Tap the heading, which shows a count like "3 of 7", to fold the list away or open it again. It goes by itself once every step is done, or after 14 days.

> **Pro tip:** do the Getting started steps in order on your first day. Each one opens the place where you do it, so the list doubles as a tour.

## Your numbers

Below the brief, Home shows numbers before actions. Results come first, and everything below is what moves them.

- **Your metrics:** your Life score, your mood and your wellbeing. Tap one for the detail.
- **Your numbers:** your day streak, xp today, your points and your level, with a bar for how far you are into it.
- **Emerging:** up to three patterns from your own logs, once there is one ready. A later lesson covers it.
- **Daily:** today''s boxes, to tick right here.
- **Up next:** events, runs and circles you joined.
- **Xp, last 14 days:** pick **By day** for a line with a green dot on each day you ticked a task, or **By source** for a ring of where your xp came from.
- **Your pillars:** a bar for each of the seven.
- **Go to:** every app you have switched on.

Some parts show only when their app is on, like Life, Knowledge and Money this month.

## Xp and levels

Xp is how the app counts what you do. A check-in pays 10 xp. A small act pays 15. A ticked task pays 20. A line under Your numbers says how much xp you need for the next level. Levels get further apart as you climb, so each one means a little more.

## The Life score

The Life score is one number out of 100. It is the average of your five areas of life: physical, financial, mental, emotional and spiritual. Each area is built from what you already log, like sleep, money, stress, mood and mindful minutes. Only you see it. The next lesson explains why the five areas come in that order.

## What emerges over time

On day one, Home is mostly empty lines. That is fine. Each check-in, task and log fills it in. After a week, the brief has real changes to talk about. After a few weeks, Emerging starts to spot patterns, and your xp line shows the shape of your weeks.

## What develops

Home does not ask you to do more. It shows you what is already true. Over time you learn to read your day at a glance, then act. That habit is what Home builds in you.

> **Result:** in one scroll you drink a glass of water, read a line, start a focus clock, check your brief and tick today''s boxes. You never leave Home.

## The bottom line

Home is the day in one place. Read the brief, do one next step, and let the numbers fill in as you go. The more you use the other apps, the more Home has to show you.', 7, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l07', 'prog-onboarding', 'Journal, trackers and the five areas of life', 'lesson', NULL, 'You can tell when a hard week hits you. You sleep late, your mood dips and your stress climbs. These apps turn that feeling into a few taps a day, so you can see it change.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to write in the Journal, and how Prompt me helps when you are stuck.
- How Mood, Sleep and Stress take a minute a day.
- How Wellbeing turns them into one score.
- How Life scores the five areas of life, and why they come in that order.

## Why these apps are there

Your pillars are about what you do. These apps are about how you are. They are all private. Your journal and your wellbeing numbers never reach a brand, the team, the leaderboard or anyone else. Even a life coach only sees your numbers if you let them, and never your journal.

## Switch them on

Journal and Life are on for every new account. Mood, Sleep, Stress and Wellbeing start off, unless the setup chat switched one on for you. To add them, open **Apps**, tap the **Reflection** group and turn on the switch beside each one.

## The Journal

1. Open **Journal** and tap **New entry**.
2. Give it a **Title**.
3. In **Say anything that is on your mind**, write as much or as little as you like.
4. Under **Emotion**, tap a face: Low, Sad, Neutral, Happy or Overjoyed.
5. Move the **Stress level** slider, and tap any **Stressor** that fits, such as Work, Money or Sleep.
6. Tap **Save entry**.

Your entries show under **My journals**. Once you have one, a calendar called **This month** shows up. Tap a past day to read it, or to write an entry for that day.

To change an entry, tap it and **Save changes**. To remove it, tap **Delete entry**. A box asks first, with a red yes.

## Prompt me

Some nights the box stays empty. Under New entry is **Not sure what to write?**

- Pick a **Category**, or leave it on Every category. There are sixteen, from Shadow work and Inner child to Gratitude and joy and Weekly reset.
- Tap **Prompt me**. A card shows a question and about how long it takes.
- Open **Why this prompt** and **What to expect** if you want to know more.
- Tap **Write about this** to start an entry with the prompt as its title.
- Tap **Another prompt** if it does not fit.

No prompt comes back until you have seen the whole set. The heavier categories end with a care note: if it gets too much, stop and talk to a professional or someone you trust.

## Mood, Sleep and Stress

Each takes about a minute.

- **Mood:** under "How are you feeling today?", tap a face and **Set mood**. Three sliders ask how active you were, how well you ate and how you slept. Then **Save today''s mood**.
- **Sleep:** under **Last night**, drag the ruler to the hours you slept and pick a **Quality**. Then **Save last night**. Seven to eight hours scores full marks.
- **Stress:** under **Right now**, move the slider from Calm to Overwhelmed. Under **What is behind it**, tap what is weighing on you. Then **Save today**.

Each app shows a line over the last 30 days. Mood also has a month calendar, so you can fill in a day you missed.

> **Pro tip:** log for one full week before you judge any score. Seven days of mood, sleep and stress make every chart honest.

## Wellbeing: one score

Wellbeing pulls the trackers into one number out of 100, from the last seven days. Mood counts most, then sleep, then stress, then your mindful minutes and your check-in streak. If you have not logged mood, sleep or stress yet, that part counts as middling, so a blank week does not drag you down. From Wellbeing you can jump to Journal to write it down, or to the Companion to talk it through.

## Life and the five areas

Life scores five areas of life, always in this order: physical, financial, mental, emotional, spiritual.

The order is the point. Each area holds up the next one. When your body is worn out, money choices get worse. When money is tight, your mind gets loud. A loud mind pulls your feelings down, and low feelings make it hard to feel any meaning. It works the other way too. A strong area lifts the one after it. So when one area slips, look at the one before it first.

Each area is built from what you already log:

- **Physical:** sleep, movement and mindful minutes.
- **Financial:** this month''s money, budgets, invoices and goals, from Finance.
- **Mental:** stress, the tasks you ticked, and your progress in School.
- **Emotional:** mood, your journal, and your friends, love and family pillars.
- **Spiritual:** stillness, small acts, and your teachers and faith pillars.

A task you tag with an area on Daily counts toward that area too. A part with nothing logged says so and does not count against you. Movement is logged right on the Life page: type the minutes under **Log movement** and tap **Add minutes**. A hundred and fifty a week gives full marks.

## What emerges over time

After a month, you see your own shape: the nights that wreck the next day, the stress that comes back every month end. Emerging, a later lesson, points at what tends to go together.

## What develops

You get better at naming how you feel. That is where change starts. You stop guessing why a week went wrong, and see which area pulled the next one down.

> **Result:** a few taps a day give you a private record of how you are, one score for your wellbeing, and a clear view of the five areas of your life.

## The bottom line

Write when you need to, log a minute a day, and let Life show you which area needs care first. Start at the front of the order, with your body, and the rest has something to stand on.', 8, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l08', 'prog-onboarding', 'Mindfulness: breathe, then focus', 'lesson', NULL, 'You sit down to work and your mind is already in five places. An hour goes by and the big task has not moved. Mindfulness gives you two simple tools for that moment: a breath to settle, and a clock to work against.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How the four tabs of Breathe and focus work.
- How a focus session is counted, kept or thrown away.
- How the clock follows you around the app.
- What your focus numbers show over time.

## Why it is there

Busy days are not the problem. Scattered days are. A few slow breaths calm your body before you start. A clock with a clear end makes one task feel small enough to begin. Together they turn "I should work on that" into "I am working on that, for 25 minutes".

## Switch it on

Mindfulness starts off on a new account. Open **Apps**, tap the **Reflection** group and turn on the switch beside **Mindfulness**. The same panel, **Breathe and focus**, also shows on Home, so you can start a clock from either place.

The page opens with your mindful minutes this week. Under it sits the panel, a dark card with four tabs: **Breathe**, **Timer**, **Pomodoro** and **Stopwatch**. Pick one, then tap the orange **Start**. While it runs, the button reads **Pause**, then **Resume**.

## Breathe

You breathe with a row of circles, one for each step of the breath. The current circle fills up as you count. Pick a **Pattern**:

- **Calm exhale** is first: in for 4, hold for 4, out for 6, rest for 2.
- **Box**, **4-7-8**, **Even**, **Triangle**, **Longer out** and **Sigh** are the others.

Pick a cue for each step: **Soft chime**, **Bell**, **Voice** or **No cue**. Choose a length with the quick buttons (1, 3, 5 or 10 minutes) or the ruler, up to 30. If the counts feel fast, tap the small "per count" button and slow each count down, up to five seconds. A finished breath always adds to your mindful minutes.

## Timer, Pomodoro and Stopwatch

- **Timer:** one countdown, no breaks. Good for a single block of work.
- **Pomodoro:** focus parts with breaks between them. Set the focus length on the ruler, from 5 to 120 minutes. Four small pickers set the **Short break**, the **Long break**, the **Sessions between long breaks** and the **Total sessions**. The card shows "Session 2 of 4" and a dot for each one. **Skip** jumps to the next part.
- **Stopwatch:** counts up from zero, for work with no set end. **Stop** keeps the whole minutes. **Reset** keeps nothing.

While a Timer or Pomodoro part runs, the **This part** buttons add or take off one or five minutes.

## Stop, keep or throw away

Life interrupts. If you tap stop on a focus part after a minute or more, the clock pauses and asks **Stop here?** You pick **Keep** with the minutes, or **Throw it away**. Close the box to carry on. A skipped part counts for nothing.

When a focus session ends, a small row asks **How focused were you?**, from Scattered to Deep. It is private, pays nothing and never changes what was kept. Tap **Skip** if you would rather not say.

## The round buttons

Five round buttons sit beside the panel''s heading:

- **Focus on a task:** pick one of your open tasks, from recent ones or a search. Its name shows on the clock and in your browser tab.
- **Timer settings:** a ready plan, **Classic 25/5**, **Deep 50/10** or **Light 15/3**, and your switches.
- **Sounds:** one switch for every chime, beat and buzz.
- **Background music:** rain, ocean, wind, a soft hum or lo-fi, with a volume.
- **Full screen:** the panel covers the page, and everything else steps aside.

In **Timer settings**, finished focus sessions count as mindful minutes to start with. Breaks do not, unless you switch that on. You can also set **A daily focus goal** and **Remind me to focus**, which sends one note to your Inbox on the days you pick. Your choices are saved to your account, so they follow you to every device.

> **Pro tip:** plan a task''s length on Daily, then tap **Start focus** there. Mindfulness opens with the task picked and the first part set to that length.

## The clock follows you

You can leave the page while a clock runs. It keeps going. A small pill shows the time left in the top corner of the screen. Its colour tells you where you are: blue with more than half left, green past halfway, orange in the last five minutes and red in the last minute. A break is violet. Tap the pill to go back to the clock.

A second tab shows the clock as "Running in another tab", so a session is never counted twice.

## Today and your numbers

Under the panel, **Today** lists your sessions, newest first. Tap one to change its length or task, or to delete it. **Continue** starts the same clock again. **Add a session I forgot** adds one for today or yesterday.

Further down, **How productive you were** shows your focus time by day, week or month, in minutes or hours, beside the tasks you ticked. **Sessions** lists your mindful minutes. Deleting one asks first.

## What emerges over time

After a few weeks you can see your real working hours, not the ones you hoped for. You see which days you focus and which you drift. Emerging may notice that on days with a focus session you tick more tasks.

## What develops

The first sessions feel long. Then 25 minutes becomes normal, and 50 feels possible. You learn to start before you feel ready. Your mindful minutes also feed the physical and spiritual areas of Life.

> **Result:** one breath to settle, one clock to work against, a task named on it, and a record of real focus that follows you to every device.

## The bottom line

Breathe first, then pick a task and start a clock. Keep what you did, throw away what you did not, and let the numbers show you how your focus grows.', 9, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l09', 'prog-onboarding', 'Reviews: look back by period', 'lesson', NULL, 'You worked hard last month. But did you move the things that matter, or only the loud ones? Days blur together. A review stops the blur. The app brings the numbers from every app you use. You bring the words.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How the four periods work, and how to write your first review.
- How templates give a review your own questions.
- How the review calendar reminds you on the day.
- How to bring a task to a review, so nothing you meant to think about gets lost.

## Why reviews are there

Daily is about today. Your pillars are about the long run. A review is the bridge between them. It asks a few plain questions once a week, a month, a quarter or half a year. Over time those answers show you how you work, not just what you did.

## Switch it on

Reviews start off on a new account. Open **Apps**, tap the **Reflection** group and turn on the switch beside **Reviews**.

## The four periods

A review covers one period:

- **Week:** Monday to Sunday. The built-in one is the **Weekly review**.
- **Month:** a calendar month, the **Monthly review**.
- **Quarter:** three months, the **Quarterly review**.
- **Half year:** six months, the **Mid-year check-in**.

You get one review per period. A period that has not started yet cannot be reviewed. Use the period control near the top to pick the kind, then the arrows to step back or forward.

## Your first review

Say you want to look back on last month.

1. Use the period control at the top. Pick **Month**, then step back to last month.
2. Under **The numbers**, each tile sits next to the month before. Tap one to go where it comes from.
3. **Day by day** shows your xp each day, and where it came from by pillar.
4. Under **Your review**, answer the questions. A few lines each is plenty.
5. Under **How did it go?**, tap a face.
6. Tap **Save this review**.

The Monthly review asks things like "What are you proud of this month?" and "What will you change next month?". The Weekly review asks "What went well this week?" and "What got in the way?".

Your review is saved with the numbers as they were that day. New data later never rewrites it. You can come back, change your words and **Save changes**. Saved reviews show under **Past reviews**. Deleting one asks first.

## Your own templates

The built-in questions always stay. You can add your own beside them.

1. Under **Templates**, tap **New template**.
2. Give it a **Name**, like "Money month".
3. In **Comes back every**, pick the period.
4. Under **Questions, one per line**, write your questions.
5. Tap **Add template**.

Next time, **Questions from** lets you choose the built-in set or yours. You can keep up to twelve templates.

## The review calendar

A review you forget to do is a review you skip. The calendar fixes that.

1. Under **Review calendar**, tap **Schedule a review**, or tap a day on the calendar.
2. Under **Which review**, pick the period.
3. Set the **First day** and the **Time**, and a **Name** if you like.
4. Under **Repeat**, pick how often: Every day, Weekdays, Every week, Every month or Every year, with an end day if you want one.
5. Tap **Schedule it**.

On that day, at that time, one note lands in your Inbox. If the day is the first of a period, the note opens the one that just ended, so a review on the 1st opens last month. On the calendar, a bell marks a reminder and a tick marks a review you wrote. Each reminder has a **Remind me** switch. You can schedule up to twenty.

> **Pro tip:** schedule the Weekly review for Sunday evening and the Monthly review for the first of the month. The note opens the right period, so starting takes one tap.

## Bring a task to the review

Some tasks are worth thinking about, not just ticking. Say you have "Raise my rates" and want to weigh it at month end.

1. On Daily, open the task''s plan.
2. Turn on **Bring it to my review**. The task now wears a **For review** tag.
3. In that period''s review, it shows under **Tasks you brought**, done or still open.
4. Tap **Add to my answer**. It adds a line to your answer and never wipes what you wrote.

A repeating task carries the flag to the next one.

## Business metrics

If you run your own business, **Business metrics** lets you track a few numbers of your own against a target each period. It stays off until you switch it on, so most people never see it.

## Who sees your reviews

Your reviews are yours. If you work with a life coach, they see saved reviews only while you leave that switch on for them, and never the numbers saved with it.

## What emerges over time

After a few weeks your answers start to repeat. The same thing "got in the way" three weeks running. That is a pattern worth acting on. Emerging, covered in a later lesson, may also show how this period compares with the one since your last review.

## What develops

You get better at telling busy from useful. You start to plan the next week from what the last one taught you. Small turns each week add up to a clear change by the half year.

> **Result:** a review for every period, your own questions, a note on the day, and the tasks you flagged waiting for you.

## The bottom line

Pick a period, answer a few questions, and schedule the next one before you close the page. A few minutes a week of looking back makes the next week better.', 10, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l10', 'prog-onboarding', 'Emerging and Discover: what your days say about you', 'lesson', NULL, 'You log a mood, tick a task, write a few lines. Put a few weeks of them side by side and they tell a story. Emerging reads it. Discover asks questions to show you how you think, feel and act.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What patterns Emerging looks for, and where they show up.
- Why it says things happen together, and never that one causes another.
- How good and bad habits are found, and what to do with each.
- How the Discover quiz and its add-ons work, as a mirror and never a diagnosis.

## Emerging: why it is there

You are too close to your own days to see their shape. Emerging steps back for you. It runs on plain rules, not AI, and needs nothing switched on.

## What it notices

It reads only apps you have on. A few things it looks for:

- **Tasks you keep doing:** the same task ticked on 3 days in 4 weeks, which does not repeat yet.
- **Sleep and mood:** your mood on days after more sleep, against days after less.
- **Focus and tasks:** how many tasks you tick on days with a focus session, and on days without.
- **Stress and calm:** your stress on days with ten mindful minutes, or with your water goal met.
- **Journal and mood:** your mood on days you wrote. It reads only when you wrote, never what.

Each card shows a number, a tag in the corner, one sentence, a small chart and a line on where it came from. It has one action, and a More menu where **Not now** hides it on this device.

## Together, never because

Every card that sets two kinds of day side by side ends with the same words: "Things that happen together, not causes." That is on purpose. Say you sleep more on days you also log a good mood. Maybe sleep lifts your mood. Maybe a calm day helps you sleep. The app cannot know which, so it never pretends to. It shows you the link. You decide what it means, because you know your life best.

## Where it shows up

- **Home:** your top three, with **See all**.
- **Daily, Pillars, Life, Journal, Reviews** and more: the patterns that touch each one.
- **The Emerging page:** two counts, **Patterns found** and **Still watching**, then **What stands out**, **Good and bad habits** and **Still watching**. A pattern still watching says what it needs, like "Log sleep and mood on 3 more days".

## Good and bad habits

This is the part people use most. Emerging takes a task you tick often, on at least 3 days in 8 weeks, and sets your days with it beside your days without it. It looks at your mood, your sleep that night, your stress, the other tasks you ticked and your focus minutes. How the day felt counts first.

- **Good habit:** the days with it went clearly better. The card offers **Make it a habit**. A sheet suggests a repeat, a first day and a time, and nothing changes until you tap **Keep**.
- **Bad habit:** the days with it went clearly worse. The card offers **Add to not to do**, which opens right in the card.
- **Still watching:** not enough days yet, or no clear gap. It needs at least 5 days with it and 7 without.

A not to do item is checked the same way, slip days against kept days. Daily shows your good and bad habits in a section called **Habits**, under the not to do list.

> **Pro tip:** keep logging mood and sleep, even on dull days. Emerging can only compare what you log, and the plain days are what make the gap clear.

## Discover: why it is there

Discover shows you how you tend to think, feel and act. It never compares you with anyone else.

Discover starts off. Switch it on in the **Reflection** group of **Apps**, or tap **Take the Discover quiz** at the end of the setup chat.

## The quiz

Tap **Start the quiz**. It has five short parts, and it saves as you go:

1. **Priorities and preferences:** three lines at a time. Pick the most and the least like you.
2. **Puzzles, part 1:** pictures to solve. They get harder or easier as you go.
3. **You, in words:** ten words. Pick three most like you and three least.
4. **Puzzles, part 2:** more of the same.
5. **Real situations:** pick the best and worst way to handle each one.

Then tap **See my strengths now**, or **Add emotional intelligence** for a few more parts.

Your result has tabs: **Strengths** (your top three of seven, and your quietest), **Personality** (five traits), **Reasoning** (where the puzzles settled, plainly not an IQ score) and **Use my results**, where you choose if it may shape Home''s next steps or help the assistant. You can change that any time.

## The add-ons

Under **More ways to know yourself** are four more self-checks: **16 personality types**, **Love languages**, **How your mind works** and **Human design**, with numerology inside it. Each is a mirror, not a verdict. The type check is not the official test. How your mind works says it is not a test for ADHD, autism or anything else. Human design and numerology are shown as ways to reflect, not as science, and they never predict.

## What emerges over time

Emerging gets sharper the more you log. Discover gives you words for what you notice. "I start many things" becomes a trait you can name, and a step you can take: finish one task first.

## What develops

You stop judging yourself by one bad week. You see what lifts your days, and choose more of it.

> **Result:** you know which habits go with your better days, you have one ready to repeat, and you have plain words for your strengths.

## The bottom line

Log the small things and let Emerging show you what goes together. Take Discover once, and use it as a mirror. Neither tells you who to be. They help you see who you already are.', 11, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l11', 'prog-onboarding', 'Connection: the people around you', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most of what you build here, you build with people. A friend who checks on you. A run club that waits for you on Sunday. The Connection apps are where that happens, and each one asks for a yes before anything moves.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to post, react and reply in Community, and when to stay anonymous.
- How Bond turns people you meet into friends.
- How events, runs and circles pay you for showing up in person.
- What the Games app is for.
- Which people make a community work, and how you might become one.

## Switch them on

The Connection apps are off on a new account, unless the setup chat turned some on for you. Open **Apps**, open the **Connection** group, and turn on the ones you want: **Community**, **Bond**, **Party**, **Kreators Run**, **Circles**, **Club** and **Games**.

## Community: posts and replies

1. Open **Community** and tap **Write a post**.
2. Under **Pick a category**, pick the one that fits, like Creative or Support.
3. Write your post and tap **Create post**.

Under any post you can react with Like, Celebrate, Support, Love, Insightful or Funny. Tap the same one again to take it back. Tap the reply count to answer. Type @ and a username to tell someone. **Show**, **Sort** and **Topic** above the feed help you find things.

Small acts here pay small amounts:

- **A post:** 5 xp, up to three a day.
- **A reply:** 2 xp, up to five a day.
- **A like you give:** 1 xp, up to ten a day.
- **A like on your post:** 1 point for you.
- **A new follower:** 2 points for you.

The pay is small on purpose. The point is to show up, not to farm.

## When to stay anonymous

Some questions are hard to ask with your name on them. Turn on **Post anonymously** before you post. Readers see a made-up name, the same all through that thread. They never see your name, your page or your followers. If someone reports a post, the team can still act on it, so anonymous never means anything goes.

Beside the feed sits **Channels**: chat rooms, one topic each. Join one to write in it, anonymously too if you like.

## Bond: from met to friends

Bond is for the people you actually know.

1. Open **Bond** and find **Your friend code**. It is eight letters and numbers.
2. Share it with a friend. They type it under **Add a friend by code** and tap **Send request**.
3. You say yes, and you are friends.

Chats in Bond always run under two made-up names, even between friends. Bond''s own **Channels** are different: small private rooms where names show, and only friends you ask can join.

Under **Introductions**, turn on **Open to introductions** to meet people from events you went to. Your details move only after the other person says yes.

## Blocking

You can block anyone, any time, from a post''s **More** menu or under a line in a channel. A block works both ways. Neither of you sees the other''s posts, replies or lines, and neither can send the other a request. The other person is not told. Lift it under **People you blocked** with **Unblock**.

## Events, runs and circles

- **Party:** meetups, workshops and bigger days. Tap **RSVP**, or **Join the waitlist** when it is full. Anyone can **Host a meetup**.
- **Kreators Run:** run clubs near you. Join one, or **Start a club** of your own. At the start line, tap **Enter the start code** to log your run.
- **Circles:** small groups that meet each week, matched by interests. Everyone at the table has a made-up name.
- **Club:** a membership you apply to. People get in on the quality of their work and how they show up, never on follower counts.

A host can set xp for guests and show an **attendee code** at the event. Type it where it says **The host''s code**. An event or circle pays up to 50 xp, once each, for up to three events in any 24 hours. A run pays up to 50 xp and 10 points, two runs a day. This xp feeds your Friends pillar, and a run''s minutes count as movement in your Life score.

## Games

**Games** holds nine card games, like FunFlow and the 7RP Challenge. You unlock one with points in **Rewards**, and it is yours for good. Tap **Draw a card** and play with the people around you.

## The people a community needs

A good community does not run itself. It needs people in a few roles:

- **Hosts** who plan a meetup or start a run club.
- **Guides** who keep Community calm and kind.
- **Life coaches** who walk beside someone for a while.
- **Mentors** who give honest feedback in School.
- **Creators** who make things others learn from.
- **Brands** who bring real work and rewards.

Anyone can host a meetup or start a club today. Guide, mentor and life coach are roles you can grow into once people know you. If you coach people already, open **Coaching** and look for **Are you a life coach?**

## What grows over time

Show up and your Friends pillar fills in. Your trust score climbs as your xp and streaks grow. Community badges arrive as you post and reply. And your Friends board on the Leaderboard starts to count you, if you opt in.

> **Pro tip:** start with one place, not seven. Join one channel or one run club and show up there every week. A small habit with people beats a big burst you cannot keep.

> **Result:** you have a friend or two in Bond, a room where you can ask for help, and a reason to leave the house each week.

## The bottom line

Connection here is built on consent. Friends need a yes, introductions need a yes, and blocks need none. Start small, show up often, and the people around you become part of your life, not just your feed.', 12, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l12', 'prog-onboarding', 'Creation and contribution: learn, make and earn', 'lesson', NULL, 'You are good at something. Maybe you edit, write, shoot or teach. The Contribution and Creation apps are where that skill turns into learning, work and income. You do not need all of them. Pick the two or three that match what you do this year.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What each app in Contribution and Creation is for, in a line or two.
- Which ones are on from the start and which you switch on.
- How work here gets checked by a real person before it pays.
- What grows as you use them.

## What is on from the start

A new account starts with **School**, **Work**, **Army** and the **Shop** on. The rest are off until you or the setup chat turn them on. Open **Apps**, then the **Contribution** or **Creation** group, and use the switch beside each app.

## Contribution: learn and earn

These apps are about giving your skill to others and getting better at it.

- **School:** cohorts with lessons, assignments, live sessions and a mentor''s feedback. Tap **Enrol** beside a cohort. You are in one right now: this program lives in School. A program priced in points asks again before it spends.
- **Work:** opportunities from brands and the team. Write **A short note** and tap **Send application**. Your public portfolio and skills go with it, one application per person. You can **Withdraw** later.
- **Army:** brand campaigns from brief to delivery. You see a brief once the team assigns you to it. Then you tap **Upload a version**, and the brand approves it or asks for changes.
- **Win:** content rewards. A brand funds a pool, you post a video about them, and verified views turn into points. Each reward has **What could I earn?** so you can see the payout first. When your video is up, tap **Submit my video**.
- **Agency:** for a content agency or a freelance creator. Clients, content pieces from idea to published, retainers, time, approvals and invoices, in one place. Each client gets their own page to approve work.

## Creation: make and sell

These apps are about making things and running your own small business.

- **Shop:** sell downloads, courses, memberships, sessions, events and more. Points work today. Paying with money is in test mode until a payment provider is connected.
- **Store:** gear new from partners, or pre-owned from other creators. Tap **Sell something** to list your old camera. Buyers make offers, and you settle the money and the handover between you. A week at the top costs 200 points.
- **Tools:** a directory of creator tools, checked by the team. **Get it** goes through a partner link, and the card says so. Know a good one? Tap **Suggest a tool**.
- **Content:** a strategist for every brand you work on. Feed a folder links, let it research, and get plans, calendars, hooks and scripts back.
- **My purchases:** everything you bought, in one list.
- **Knowledge:** short reads and exercises from the guides. Each takes a few minutes.
- **TV** and **Fund:** pitch a series, or apply for backing for a project.

## A course of your own

Say you cut reels that keep people watching, and friends keep asking how. In the **Shop**, you can turn that into a course.

1. Tap **New product** and pick **Course**.
2. Add a title and a price in points, or zero to make it free.
3. Add your lessons.
4. Choose who can see it.

A course has four states. **Draft** is only for you. **Private** is only for the people you invite. **Public** is open to anyone. **Archived** is hidden, and buyers keep what they bought. Start private with a few friends, fix what confuses them, then make it public.

You can also ask for a lock: some xp, some points, or a badge before someone can buy. Locks reward the people who show up.

## A person checks before anything pays

Nothing here pays on a claim alone. A mentor reads your hand-in in School. A brand approves your version in Army. A reviewer checks your views in Win. Money from a sale is paid only once the payment provider confirms it. This is slower than a button that pays at once. It is also why the points and the work here mean something.

## The roles you grow into

These apps need people on both sides. Learners need mentors. Brands need creators. Buyers need sellers they trust. As you deliver good work, you may be asked to mentor a cohort, run campaigns or teach a course. Nobody is born into these roles. They come from showing up and doing the work well.

## What grows over time

- **Your trust score:** sales and good ratings feed it, beside your xp and streaks. Buyers see it on your products and listings.
- **Your badges:** the Creator family counts products, sales and content rewards. The Learning family counts lessons finished and certificates earned.
- **Your Servitude pillar:** its question is "Who did you serve today?" A client, a learner or a brand counts. Put that work in its pyramid as projects and tasks.
- **You:** a skill shared becomes a skill sharpened. Teaching what you know is one of the fastest ways to learn it better.

> **Pro tip:** do not switch on every app here at once. Pick one way to learn and one way to earn. Add a third only when those two feel easy.

> **Result:** you know which apps fit your work, you have one switched on to learn and one to earn, and you know who checks your work before it pays.

## The bottom line

Contribution and Creation are where your skill meets other people. Learn in School, earn in Work, Army and Win, and build your own thing in the Shop and Agency. Start with two, do them well, and let your trust grow with each piece you deliver.', 13, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l13', 'prog-onboarding', 'Xp, points, badges and your money', 'lesson', NULL, 'You have been checking in for a few weeks. Xp, points, a streak and a few badges are piling up. What are they for? This lesson sorts them out, then shows you Finance, the private book for your real money.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The difference between xp and points, and how each is earned.
- What points can buy, from rewards to card games.
- How badges and honors work, and which ones stay private.
- How the Leaderboard works, and why it is your choice.
- What your trust score is made of.
- How Finance keeps your real money, in your own currency.

## Xp and points are not the same

- **Xp shows effort.** You earn it by doing things: a check-in, the small act, a ticked task, a post, an event. It moves your level, your stage and your pillars.
- **Points can be spent.** You earn them more slowly, and you trade them for real things.

Here is what the everyday things pay:

- **Check in:** 10 xp and 10 points. The first box on Daily.
- **A week in a row:** every seventh day of a check-in streak pays 50 points more.
- **The small act:** 15 xp.
- **A task:** 20 xp. Six a day pay, and the rest still count as done.
- **A kept day on the not to do list:** 5 xp.
- **Clearing a project:** 30 xp, when every task in it is done.

Posts, events and runs pay a little more. The full list is in **Help**, under **How you earn**.

Your stage grows with xp and days together. You start as a Seed, become a Sprout at 300 xp and 3 days, and grow on up to Luminary. A stage cannot be rushed.

## What points buy

On the **Profile** tab, find **Points**. It has two buttons: **Rewards** and **Rank**.

1. Tap **Rewards**. **Your balance** shows at the top.
2. Scroll the **Catalog**. Its shelves include Books, Games, Apparel and wearables, Tech accessories and Experiences.
3. Tap **Reserve** on a thing you want. A sheet asks again before any points move.
4. It shows under **Your reservations**. Gear stays a reservation until a drop ships.

The **Games** shelf is different. A card game opens at once and is yours for good, so it cannot be cancelled. Some games also ask for some xp first, and a locked one says what is missing.

Points also pay for things priced in points in the Shop, School and Party, and for a featured week in the Store.

## Badges and honors

Badges are medals the app gives by its own count. Find them on the **Profile** tab, under **Badges**.

- **About sixty badges** in ten families, from Daily habits to Milestones.
- **Four tiers:** bronze, silver, gold and platinum.
- **One rule each:** a single count reaching a number. Tap a badge to see its next step and a bar.
- **Kept for good:** once earned, the app never takes a badge back on its own (only Erase everything clears them).
- **Some stay private:** the Reflection, Focus and Money families show only to you. They never appear on your page or the Leaderboard.

A badge pays nothing, but some products, games and tools ask for one before they open.

Honors are different. They come from time in the app, like One week in and One year in, or from the team. They show under **Awarded**.

## The Leaderboard is your choice

The Leaderboard is on for everyone, but you are not on it until you say so.

1. On the **Profile** tab, tick **Show me on the leaderboard**.
2. Tap **Save profile**.
3. Tap **Rank** under Points to open it.

Pick a **Board**: Everyone, By goal, By pillar, By city, By who they are or By experience. Then pick **Rank by**: All time, This month, Streak or Badges. Each month is a season, and its top three get the Season top three honor.

Your journal, wellbeing and date of birth are never used. Nobody you blocked, and nobody who blocked you, is listed.

## Trust: what your work proves

Your trust score is one number out of a hundred. It reads New here, Getting known, Trusted or Well trusted. It is built only from what you earned: xp, points earned, your best streak, time here, sales and their rating, and honors. Nothing in it can be typed in. People see it on your public page, your products and your pre-owned listings. Tap it to see what it is made of.

## Finance: your real money

Points are the app''s. Finance is for your own money. It is off on a new account, in the **Creation** group. Only you can see it.

1. First, pick your currency. Open the **Public page** app, find **Currency** and save. It is rupees unless you change it.
2. Open **Finance**. Tap **Money in** or **Money out**, type the amount and who it was with, and save.
3. Use **Owed** for money someone owes you, or you owe them. **Monthly** posts a bill on the same day each month. **Goal** saves toward something. **Budgets** watches your spending.

Finance adds up only your currency. Other currencies are listed but not added in. If you use Agency, your sent invoices show as owed to you.

## What grows over time

Your level climbs, your badges fill in family by family, and your trust grows as you keep showing up. Finance turns a messy month into one clear line: what came in, what went out and what you kept.

> **Pro tip:** check in every day for your first week before you spend a single point. The seventh day pays the bonus, and you will know what you actually want by then.

> **Result:** you know what xp and points are for, you have reserved something real, you chose whether to be on the Leaderboard, and your money has one private home.

## The bottom line

Xp shows effort, points buy things, badges mark the road, and trust grows from what you prove. None of it can be bought or typed in. Show up, spend on what you will use, and keep your real money in Finance, where only you can see it.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l14', 'prog-onboarding', 'The assistant and the Companion', 'lesson', NULL, 'Some days you want to say "log my sleep and add a task for Friday" and be done. Other days you just want to talk the day through. The assistant does the first. The Companion does the second.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What the assistant does for free, and what needs a plan.
- How plans and credits work, in plain words.
- How every change can be undone.
- What the Companion is, and what it is not.
- Where to turn if you are ever in danger.

## Meet the assistant

The round button with Bumling in it, at the bottom right of every screen, is the assistant. On a phone it sits above the tab bar. Tap it, and the **Bumling AI assistant** panel opens. The page under it stays in view, so you can watch things land.

**Close** folds the panel back into the button and keeps your chat.

## The free half: flows

Flows are short taps that cost nothing. Each one says where its answer lands. A few of them:

- **Check in for today** and **Mark the small act done**, which land on Daily.
- **Add a task** and **Add to a pyramid**, which land in Pillars.
- **Mark my not to do list** and **Add to my not to do list**.
- **Log my mood**, **Log my sleep**, **Log my stress** and **Log mindful minutes**.
- **Write in my journal**, which only you can see.
- **Add money in or out** and **Set a money goal**, when Finance is on.

A flow for an app that is off stays hidden. Flows use the same steps as your own taps, so the same rules apply.

## The paid half: typing

Typing a line, like "move my edit task to Friday", needs a plan.

1. Type what you want in the box at the bottom.
2. Bumling does it in the app, as you, with the same rules as your own taps.
3. Each change comes back as a row with **Undo**.
4. Removing something, or settling money owed, waits for you to tap **Confirm**.
5. While it works, the send arrow turns into a **Stop** square. What was already done stays, each with its Undo.

Each typed reply says **Answered by** and the model''s name, so you always know it came from a model and not a built-in line.

Without a plan, you can still type a question about a word, like "what is a layer?", and get a plain answer.

## Privacy and the model

When a model is connected, a model picker shows under the panel''s header. Some models keep nothing you send. Only with one of those can the assistant read your journal and your trackers, and it reads the journal only when you ask about it. The panel tells you under the picker what the chosen model may read.

If you took the Discover quiz, you choose whether the assistant learns from it. Find **Let your results help** on your Discover results.

## Plans and credits, in plain words

Open the **Profile** tab and tap **Plans and credits**.

- **A credit** is a tiny slice of what the model costs to run, worked out from the words it reads and writes.
- **A plan** gives you a set number of credits for 30 days. Pro is ₹1,499 for 10,000 credits, Pro Max is ₹12,499 for 100,000, and Ultra is ₹49,999 for 400,000. Credits left at the end of a plan do not carry over.
- **A top up** adds extra credits on top of a plan. They are used after the plan''s own and stay until you use them.

The page shows your plan, what you used today and every credit in and out. Some free models cost no credits at all, but typing still needs a plan. Payments are not live yet, so picking a plan charges nothing, and the page tells you so.

## Meet the Companion

The Companion is a chat that knows how you are doing. It is not a therapist. It lives in the **Reflection** group and starts off, so turn it on under **Apps**.

1. Open **Companion**.
2. Tap a starter, like "I feel tired today", or type in **Say what is on your mind**.
3. It answers in a few warm, short lines.

With a plan and credits left, a model answers. Without them, the built-in companion answers for free. You never hit a paywall here. Each line says who answered. The Companion only reads: it never adds, ticks or removes anything for you.

Your mood, sleep and wellbeing reach only a model that keeps nothing. To clear the chat, tap the trash button. A sheet asks first, because it cannot be undone.

## If you are ever in danger

The Companion watches for words that sound like danger, before any model sees them. If it hears them, it tells you to talk to a person right now. If you are in immediate danger, call your local emergency number or go to the nearest hospital. In India you can call Tele-MANAS on 14416, at any hour. Tell someone you trust too.

kreators.app cannot help in an emergency. A real person can.

## What grows over time

The more you use the app, the more the assistant has to work with, always as you and never past your own rules. The Companion reads your day and your routine, so its answers fit. And you get time back for the things only you can do.

> **Pro tip:** start with the free flows for a week. When you notice you keep wishing you could just type it, that is the moment a plan is worth it.

> **Result:** you log a whole evening in one panel, undo anything you did not mean, and have a quiet place to talk when the day was heavy.

## The bottom line

The flows are free for everyone. Typing needs a plan, and credits pay for the model. Every change has an Undo, and nothing leaves your own rules. The Companion listens, and never charges you to talk. And in danger, a real person always comes first.', 15, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l15', 'prog-onboarding', 'Your privacy and your data', 'lesson', NULL, 'You write things here that you would not post: how you slept, what stressed you, a page in your journal. So you should know who sees what. Most of it is yours alone, and you hold the switches for the rest.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What nobody else ever sees.
- What others may see, and the switch for each part.
- How your devices and your password work.
- How to take your data with you, or wipe it.

## What only you see

Some things in the app are for your eyes only. They never reach brands, the team, the leaderboard or your public page.

- **Your journal:** every entry, every prompt you wrote about. Not even a coach sees it.
- **Your wellbeing logs:** mood, sleep, stress and mindful minutes, and the one wellbeing score made from them. Only a coach you said yes to can see the numbers, and only while you allow it.
- **Discover:** your quiz answers, your results and the add-ons, like love languages and how your mind works.
- **Your phone and date of birth:** asked once at sign-up, never shown to anyone.
- **Finance:** your money in and money out. The screen says it: "Only you can see this."
- **Your year:** the grid of days on the Profile tab.
- **Emerging:** the patterns the app notices. They are worked out fresh each time you look, and never saved.

Your tags, the words the setup chat gave you, pick your apps. They never show on your public page. Some badges are private too. The Focus, Reflection and Money families show only to you, and only counts are used for them, never what you wrote.

## What others may see, and your switch for it

### Your public page

Your public page is your front door. On the Profile tab, **See my public page** shows it and **Edit page** changes it.

Under **What is public** there are ten switches. **My page is public** comes first. Turn it off and the whole page hides from everyone but you. The rest need it on:

- **Show my social icons**, **Show my links**, **Show my portfolio** and **Show what I sell**.
- **Show my posts** and **Show my badges**.
- **Show my activity:** your level, streak and how many pillars are set up. This one starts off.
- **Let people add me as a friend** and **Let friends start a chat**.

Tap **Save profile** when you are done.

### The leaderboard

You are not on it until you tick **Show me on the leaderboard** on the Profile tab. Your journal and wellbeing never count there.

### A life coach

If you work with a coach, you pick what they see, in five parts: Daily and streaks, pillars and pyramids, routine and contract, life map answers, and reflection. All five start on. Reflection is your wellbeing numbers and saved reviews, never your journal or a tracker''s note. Turn any part off, or end the link, at any time.

### The assistant

The assistant reads your journal only when you ask it about your journal. Even then, only a model that keeps nothing may read it, and the picker marks those models "keeps nothing". Discover reaches it only as a short summary, and only if you switch on **Let the assistant learn from them**.

### People you talk to

- **Post anonymously:** in Community you can post or reply under a made-up name. Others never see who you are.
- **Block:** in Bond, block anyone. Neither of you sees the other''s posts or friend asks. **People you blocked** lets you lift it later.
- **No live links:** a web address in a post or a chat shows as plain text.

## Your privacy choices

The first time you visit, a small card called **Your privacy** asks about three things. All three start off.

- **Remember which link brought me:** a cookie for thirty days that tells a creator which link sent you.
- **Show videos from YouTube and Vimeo:** off means a video waits until you tap it.
- **Send me news and offers by email:** off means only the mail you need, like receipts.

**Reject all** and **Accept all** look the same, so neither is pushed. **Choose for each** opens the three switches. To change your mind later, open **Privacy choices** on the Profile tab. Each tick saves at once.

## Your devices and your password

- **Three devices at once:** your account can be open on three phones or laptops. Sign in on a fourth and the oldest one is signed out. That device then says why.
- **Change password:** on the Profile tab, under Account. Every other device is then signed out.
- **Forgot it:** the sign-in screen has **Forgot your password?** It mails you a link that works for thirty minutes.
- **Your calendar:** under **Calendar**, **Show my tasks in my calendar** starts off. It shows task titles, pillars, priorities and times, never your notes or journal. **Make a fresh link** stops the old one.

> **Pro tip:** if you lose a phone, change your password from any other device. That one step signs the lost phone out.

## Take it with you, or wipe it

On the Profile tab, the **Your data** card has three buttons.

1. **Export my data:** everything we hold about you, as one file you can keep.
2. **Erase everything:** your pillars, xp, streak and badges go, and you start over. A sheet asks first, with a red **Yes, erase everything** and **Keep my progress**. After it, the setup chat opens again.
3. **Delete my account:** a sheet lists what goes. You type DELETE, then tap **Delete everything**. Your name, page, pillars, logs, journal and messages are gone. Money records stay, without your name.

> **Result:** you know what is yours alone, which switch shares each part, and how to leave with your data.

## The bottom line

Your inner life stays yours: journal, wellbeing, Discover and your tags. Everything you share has a switch you own. And you can take your data with you, or wipe it, at any time.', 16, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l16', 'prog-onboarding', 'Switching apps on and off', 'lesson', NULL, 'kreators.app holds about forty apps. Nobody needs them all. A student needs different ones from a video editor. So every app has a switch, and the app works best when you keep only the ones you use.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How the apps are grouped, and which ones are always there.
- How to switch an app on or off, and what happens to your data.
- How to choose which groups start open, and put apps in your own order.
- Why fewer apps often means more grows.

## The five groups

Every app lives in one group. The groups always come in the same order, on every screen.

- **Contribution:** work for others and for pay, like School, Work, Army, Win and Agency.
- **Reflection:** looking inward, like Journal, Life, Mood, Sleep, Stress, Mindfulness, Reviews, Discover and the Companion.
- **Connection:** people, like Community, Bond, Party, Kreators Run, Circles, Club and Games.
- **Creation:** making and selling, like your Public page, Shop, Store, Finance, Knowledge and Tools.
- **Essentials:** the Leaderboard, Inbox and Help.

A few parts have no switch at all, because the app is built on them. Home, Daily, Pillars, Search and the assistant are always on, so they do not show in the lists.

## Where your first apps came from

You did not start with a blank list. A new account comes with a few apps on, like School, Work, Journal, Life and Shop. Then the setup chat read your answers and switched on the apps that fit you, up to five. It switched off the other apps it can switch, so your screen starts calm.

That was a first guess, not a rule. You can change it at any time, in two places.

## The Apps page

The **Apps** tab is the quick way.

1. Open **Apps**. The line at the top says how many apps are on.
2. Each group is one big card that says something like "3 of 7 on". Tap it to open it.
3. Each app shows its icon, its name and one line on what it does.
4. Tap the switch at the right to turn the app on or off.
5. When an app is on, tap its name to open it.

## Profile, Features

The **Features** part of the Profile tab shows the same groups, all open. It adds three things the Apps page does not have.

- **Fits you:** an app that matches your setup answers says so, with the reason. It is a hint, never a push.
- **Starts open on Apps and in the menu:** each group has this switch. Turn it on for the groups you use most. By default, Contribution and Essentials start open, and the rest start closed.
- **Arrange apps:** tap it and each app gets a drag handle, plus **Move up** and **Move down** buttons. Put the app you use most at the top. Apps move only inside their own group. Tap **Done** when you are happy.

A group you arranged also gets a **Reset order** button, in case you want the old order back.

> **Pro tip:** your order and your open groups are saved on your account, not on one phone. Sign in on a laptop and the sidebar is already set up your way.

## What happens when you switch an app off

Off hides an app. Nothing is deleted.

- Your pages, logs and tasks stay put. Turn the app back on and they are there.
- Some things still need an app to be on. A pattern on Emerging, for example, only runs while every app it reads is on. Switch Sleep off and the app stops looking for links with your sleep.

If you want your data gone, that is a different step: **Erase everything** or **Delete my account** under Your data on the Profile tab. The privacy lesson covers both.

## Where your apps show up

Once an app is on, it appears in a few places at once.

- **On a laptop:** the sidebar lists your apps under their groups. The round button on its edge folds it to a slim row of icons, and opens it again.
- **On a phone:** the button at the top right opens **Your apps**, the same groups in a menu.
- **On Home:** the **Go to** section lists every app you have switched on, with **All apps** to open the Apps page.

Inside a group, the order follows you. Your own order comes first. After that come the apps that are on, then the ones that fit your answers, then the ones you open most on this device.

Every app''s main screen ends with a **How it works** card. Tap it to read the steps and the words the app uses. **Help** has every word in one place, under **Words we use**.

## Run the setup chat again

Life changes. Maybe you started freelancing, or you joined a run club. On the Profile tab, under **Who you are**, tap **Change my tags and apps**. The setup chat opens with your last answers filled in. Change what is different, and it suggests a fresh set of apps.

Read its list before you say yes. Like the first time, it switches off the other apps it can switch. Your data in them stays safe.

Some apps also turn on by themselves when you earn them. Unlock a card game in Rewards, for example, and **Games** switches on.

## Why fewer is better

Each app you add asks for some of your time. Three to five apps you open every week beat fifteen you forget. Start small, and add an app when you need it.

> **Result:** your screen shows the apps you use, in the groups you open, in the order you chose, on every device.

## The bottom line

Every app has a switch, and off never deletes. Use the Apps page to switch fast, and Profile, Features to set which groups open and in what order. Keep the list short, and let it grow with you.', 17, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-onboarding-l17', 'prog-onboarding', 'How to keep going', 'lesson', NULL, 'Starting is easy. The hard part is week three, when the newness wears off. This last lesson is a plan for your first week and month, and a look at what grows when you stay.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- A simple rhythm for your first seven days.
- What to do at the end of a week and a month.
- What slowly grows in the app, and in you.
- What to do after a bad week.

## Your first week

Keep it small. The aim is to show up, not to do everything.

1. **Open Daily each day and tap I showed up today.** It pays 10 xp and 10 points, and the button then reads "Checked in, day 1", then day 2, and so on.
2. **Do the small act.** Your weakest pillar gets a two-minute act each day, for 15 xp. It is your easiest win.
3. **Tick one or two tasks.** A task pays 20 xp, and six a day pay. A task cannot be ticked before its do date. After your first three ticks, a task must be ten minutes old, and ten minutes after your last tick.
4. **Pick one thing for your not to do list.** One thing you want to stay away from, like scrolling in bed. Each kept day pays 5 xp, and the run of kept days is the real prize.
5. **Follow Getting started on Home.** For your first 14 days it lists a few first steps. Each one ticks itself when you do the thing.

On the seventh day of your check-in streak you get 50 points more. That bonus comes again every seventh day.

> **Pro tip:** at the start of a week, tap **Plan my week** under Tasks on Daily. It suggests a day for each task that has none, and nothing changes until you tap **Keep what fits**.

## The end of a week

Switch on **Reviews** in the Reflection group if it is not on yet. Then look back.

1. Open the week that just ended. The app brings your numbers from every app you use, next to the week before.
2. Answer four short questions: what went well, what got in the way, which pillar needs you most, and one thing to do differently.
3. Tap **Save this review**. It is saved with the numbers as they were.

A task you marked **Bring it to my review** waits under **Tasks you brought**. To make this a habit, tap **Schedule a review**, pick a day and a time, and the inbox reminds you.

## What grows over the weeks

### Your pyramids fill

Each pillar is a pyramid that stands on its point. When you tick a task, its glow runs up through the project, the goal outcome, the value goal and the guiding principle to the foundation. Each layer above gets a little xp too. Finish every task in a project and you get 30 xp more.

### Your streaks and your year

Your check-in streak counts the days in a row. On Home, **Xp, last 14 days** shows your last two weeks. On the Profile tab, **Your year** fills in a grid of days, darker on your busier ones. Only you see it.

### Who you are becoming

Daily and Profile both show **Who you are becoming**. Each form needs xp and time: Seed, then Sprout at 300 xp and 3 days, Climber at 900 xp and 14 days, Steady hand at 2,000 xp and 45 days, and Luminary at 4,000 xp and 120 days. Time is part of it on purpose, so nobody rushes it in a weekend.

### Badges

About sixty badges in ten families arrive by their own count, like First tick, Three in a row and Full week. A badge pays nothing. Once the app gives you one, it never takes it back on its own.

### Patterns on Emerging

After a few weeks, Emerging starts to speak. It might notice a task you keep coming back to and offer **Make it a habit**. It might see that your mood is higher on days you tick "Evening walk". That needs at least five days with the walk and seven without. Until then, it sits under **Still watching**, with what it still needs. Every line says things happen together, never that one causes the other.

### Trust

Your public page carries a trust score. It grows from your xp, points, best streak, time here and more. Nothing in it can be typed in. It only grows by doing.

## The end of your first month

1. **Write the month''s review.** The questions ask what you are proud of, what slid and what you will change.
2. **Look at Life.** The five areas of life always go physical, financial, mental, emotional, spiritual. Each one holds up the next, so a weak one pulls down the one after it. Start with the first weak one.
3. **Check your life map.** On Pillars, if your life has moved, tap **Run the session again**. Or tap **Build the pillars for your focus** when you are ready for more.
4. **Tidy your apps.** Switch off what you did not open this month.

## After a bad week

You will miss days. Your streak goes back to zero, and that is all. Your xp, your badges and your pyramids stay.

- Start with the smallest thing: the check-in and the two-minute small act.
- Look at Home''s daily brief. Its **Next steps** pick a few things that fit where you are today.
- A slip on your not to do list ends that run. Tick tomorrow and a new run starts.

> **Result:** you have a plan for a week, a month and a bad week. And you know what slowly grows: pyramids, streaks, badges, patterns and trust.

## The bottom line

kreators.app is seven relationships played as a daily game. You set them up once, then show up a little each day. Daily holds the small steps. Pillars hold the big picture. Reflection shows you what is changing, and Connection puts you among people who are growing too. None of it needs to be perfect. Come back tomorrow, tick one thing, and let the rest grow.', 18, 1);
